  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">KH-CIPO_ Our IP&apos;s identity, Our rights!</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to change the perspective of policymakers to pay stronger attention, show commitment, and accept recommendations from IPs and their institutions on the revisions of the current laws that affect IPs&#x2019; identity and their community. It is indented to see the IP alliance join their collective action to raise their voices in public and in consultation forums without intimidation and harassment. Leading by CIPO, the collective demand of 7 IP organizations (1. Cambodian Indigenous Women&#x2019;s Association (CIWA), 2. Cambodian Indigenous Youth Association (CIYA), and 3. Indigenous,  4. IP Human Rights Defenders (IPHRD), 5. Conserve Indigenous Peoples Languages (CIPL), 6. Indigenous Agriculture and Development in Cambodia (IADC), and 7. Organization for the Promotion of Kui Culture (OPKC)) and their alliance is reflected in the adopted laws that affect their communities. Key actions of the join alliance to address these issues include 1). Establish an indigenous legal team capable of addressing policygaps in the draft laws, composed of community experts on the expected impact, technical legal specialists (indigenous and non-indigenous), and Indigenous Law students. 2). Produce an in-depth analysis of the legal gaps that are present in the revised draft laws that affect IPs&#x2019; identity and community, and produce a common statement as evidence-based documentation that can be used for advocacy based on guided advocacy strategies.3). Organize formal and informal advocacy dialogues/campaigns to have their recommendations and inputs incorporated into the amended or new policies/laws. 4). Mobilize indigenous communities on the ground to provide testimonies about existing laws&apos; effects and provide context, evidence, and information for advocacy with legislative bodies. 5). Support advocacy campaigns/initiatives organized by affected communities for awareness-raising (for instance, through the media) and for addressing the issues. And last but not least, seek valuable support from important international and regional actors (AIPP, ILC, UN agencies, etc.) to influence relevant stakeholders in the policy-making process.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CIPO is a new established and registered organization (2014), is an IP-led organization that combines the best knowledge of IP and Cambodian society context with the ability to creatively respond to the needs, for supports, of the IPs in Cambodia,. The organization is execute and operating by different ethnic groups of young generation and under active support from eldest group who as advisory board. CIPO has it governance board consists of leaders from local and international organizations. CIPO established to build the capacity and empower the IPs to take lead and own the development processes of IP communities in Cambodia.

CIPO has a clear vision to see IPs have self-determination on development and conservation within the multi-cultural society ofCambodia. And its mission is to strengthen the capacity and support strategic initiative of the IPs in land and natural resources management, conservation of identity and culture, and socio/economic development.

The core values that lead our action are:
- Leadership. We believe in the potential of others (especially women and youth) to develop and define their own direction.
- Integrity and Accountability. We value integrity and we believe in holding ourselves accountable to all stakeholders.
- Non-discrimination. We value equitable relationships between peoples regardless of culture/tradition, ethnicity, status, hierarchy, gender, age, political trend or background.
- Solidarityand Sacrifice. We value solidarity and we sacrifice for a common spirit, collective benefits, and commitment to develop and sustainthe IP community.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cambodia Indigenous Peoples Organiz</narrative>
